{"reference":"Jeremiah 4","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Voices and catastrophe","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 19–31 portray disaster through intensely embodied and world-shaping images: inner anguish, alarms of war, ruined tents and cities, a land returned to waste and darkness, and Daughter Zion gasping in labor-like pain. The passage also shifts among divine speech, a first-person anguished voice, communal language, and the personified city. These changing voices and images present catastrophe as something experienced across personal, communal, and even cosmic dimensions rather than as a detached report.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 4:19-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_004_6ebc25bad9f1fe95138f5c86"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}